# Direct dependencies for the backend image, hand-edited.
#
# requirements.txt is GENERATED from this file (plus sync/requirements.in as
# a cross-project constraint) and must never be hand-edited: it is the full,
# hash-pinned transitive closure, produced by scripts/compile-requirements.sh.
# See that script's header for how and when to regenerate, and
# README.md#dependency-lockfiles for the short version.
#
# Every line below is a package this backend imports or invokes directly.
# Anything pulled in only transitively (starlette, anyio, h11, certifi,
# pydantic-core, ...) belongs in requirements.txt, never here.

# FastAPI and web framework
#
# Bumped from 0.109.2 straight to the 0.141.x line rather than the first
# starlette advisory fix (0.115.x) because fastapi's own pin on starlette
# stays a floor with no ceiling from 0.115.6 onward (`starlette<0.42.0` up to
# 0.115.x, `>=0.40.0` after) -- a partial bump still leaves 6 of 7 published
# starlette advisories open. 0.141.1 requires `starlette>=0.46.0`, which
# resolves to 1.6.0 and clears all 7. See pip-audit output referenced in the
# PR description for the before/after.
fastapi==0.141.1
uvicorn[standard]==0.27.1
python-multipart==0.0.32

# Database
asyncpg==0.29.0
sqlalchemy[asyncio]==2.0.25
alembic==1.13.1

# Redis
redis==5.0.1

# Authentication
#
# Was python-jose[cryptography]==3.5.0. jose pulls in `ecdsa` for its
# pure-Python backend even when the `cryptography` extra is installed, and
# `ecdsa` carries PYSEC-2026-1325 with no fix the maintainers intend to ship
# (they consider the side-channel out of scope). PyJWT does not depend on
# `ecdsa` at all -- HS256, this app's default JWT_ALGORITHM, uses only the
# stdlib `hmac`/`hashlib`. The `crypto` extra is kept anyway, the same
# way jose's `cryptography` extra was: nothing here signs with an asymmetric
# algorithm today, but JWT_ALGORITHM is an unconstrained string
# (backend/app/core/config.py), and switching it to RS256/ES256 without a
# dependency already in place would fail at import time instead of at
# start-up. It arrives pinned only through this extra, the same as it
# previously arrived only through jose's -- there is no separate
# `cryptography==` line.
PyJWT[crypto]==2.13.0
# Pinned exactly, not >=. A rebuild on 2026-08-30 silently moved this from
# 4.x to 5.0.0 -- a major version, in the login path (core/security.py calls
# bcrypt.checkpw on every request to /api/auth/token), with no review. It was
# fine, but it was luck. 5.0.0 is the version the 199-test suite was run
# against, so it is the version deployed.
bcrypt==5.0.0
# Forced up from 2.6.1 by fastapi==0.141.1, which requires pydantic>=2.9.0.
# 2.13.5 is the version that single pip resolve (this file + sync/requirements.in)
# actually lands on; pinned exactly rather than left to float, same as
# everything else in this file.
pydantic[email]==2.13.5

